WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.380
Sherrod Sloan Filmmaker/LA Film School Student:
My question was how do we approach transmedia

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:05.140
as an independent filmmaker?

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.769
A lot of us are working on tight budgets and
low resources and are having a hard enough

00:00:09.769 --> 00:00:12.009
time trying to create our one story.

00:00:12.009 --> 00:00:14.559
How do we approach transmedia?

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:18.330
And should we be trying to execute all these
projects are just develop them?

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:23.029
Houston Howard, Transmedia Author and Instructor:
This is a question I get a lot.

00:00:23.029 --> 00:00:25.760
How does this apply to the independent filmmaker.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:32.009
How does this apply to someone who is not
Marvel or STAR WARS or Harry Potter?

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:39.250
And I think that while I love STAR WARS and
GAME OF THRONES and Marvel and all the big

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:44.440
stuff, I think this is more necessary for
the independent filmmaker then even those

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.379
big brands.

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:49.333
Primarily because the independent filmmakers
don’t have the P and A [prints and advertising]

00:00:49.333 --> 00:00:54.739
budgets that large movies have and they don’t
have the brand awareness that large brands

00:00:54.739 --> 00:00:56.469
already have.

00:00:56.469 --> 00:01:04.190
If you’re an independent filmmaker operating
on a $100,000 feature, a $20,000 feature or

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:10.160
doing something even more shoestring (the
$7,000 feature), you have to figure out every

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.090
single way possible to build audience.

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:20.400
You have to figure out every single way possible
to compete in a marketplace with not just

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:28.170
Goliaths like STAR WARS and Marvel but everything
competes with everything now.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:38.010
And that’s where we are getting this with
this entertainment landscape is it’s commoditized,

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:43.640
over-saturated and the competition is just
competition for eyeballs.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.720
And so what’s really interesting Netflix
(the CEO at Netflix) says their biggest competition

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:51.390
isn’t Amazon or Hulu or HBO.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:55.570
Their biggest competition is Fortnight and
they are consistently losing to Fortnight

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:57.130
which is a video game.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:59.250
And so why do they say that?

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:04.700
It’s because Fornite even though it’s
in a different industry necessarily from video

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:08.340
games to film to television is just taking
away eyeballs.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:13.450
And so now filmmakers today (especially indie
filmmakers) are competing not just against

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:18.950
the other films that are in the market or
that are in the festival market or films that

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.950
are streaming, films that are in the theater.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:25.090
They are competing against the films that
are in the theater, the films that are streaming,

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:29.730
every film every made and anything that’s
on TV and anything that’s on Apple Music

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:36.280
or Spotify, any podcast that’s on the podcast
store, any video game on Steam or on Xbox,

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.420
Playstation on mobile.

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:43.100
You are competing against Youtube, Instagram,
you are competing against everything because

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.610
everything is in competition with everyone’s
eyeballs.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:52.960
You are competing for the attention for the
consumer or the audience.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:55.540
Attention is the game.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:59.030
That’s the thing that everyone is fighting
for.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:03.460
It’s like the Game of Thrones, everyone
is fighting for the Iron Throne.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:06.990
In entertainment everyone is fighting for
the attention of the audience.

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:11.990
And there is so many things pulling attention
now it’s a tougher fight.

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:15.000
This has never been like this ever.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.400
But studios they release films around new
video game releases.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.170
When the new Halo video game is going to drop
the studios avoid that weekend because they

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:29.880
know anyone 18 to 30 is going to be playing
Halo all weekend.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.440
They are not going to be going to the box
office.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:36.860
All that (to get back to saying) if you are
the small independent producer that has a

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:42.650
low budget you have to figure out a way to
compete, you have to figure out a way to survive,

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:48.750
to thrive, to exist in this market and it’s
tough to out punch everybody.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:54.230
You can’t overpower STAR WARS, you can’t
overpower Fortnight.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:56.140
Now you have to be craftier right?

00:03:56.140 --> 00:04:02.840
You can’t just inundate the world with marketing,
with posters, with trailers, and buy TV spots.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.860
So what can you do?

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:10.450
If you have a good idea and you have a great
story and you’re a great storyteller now

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:16.970
there all these really interesting tools that
we can now use to send your story out and

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:19.010
capture the attention of the audience.

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:23.690
It’s not really about putting one fishing
lore out there.

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:31.240
Now that we have so many tools we can have
10 or 12 simultaneously fishing in several

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:36.440
ponds all the time looking for audience.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:41.350
What I get though is independent creators
saying 1) I don’t know how to do it or if

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:48.220
they know how to do it they say “That seems
like a lot of work.”

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:55.900
To me that is such the wrong mindset to have
because we talked about this in the first

00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:02.180
round of interviews we did is I always encourage
independent filmmakers to be thinking as entrepreneurs

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:11.250
not just an artist because when you are creating
IP [intellectual property], when you are launching

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.430
a film you are launching a small business.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:17.620
You are setting up an LLC [Limited Liability
Company], you are staffing it with crew and

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:20.090
you are creating something.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:25.360
You have to think about it just like the guy
who is opening up the coffee shop or the woman

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:29.460
who is opening up the pizza shop or the family
who is opening up the candle store down the

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:32.389
street, you have to have that same mentality.

00:05:32.389 --> 00:05:37.949
And so if you understand the amount of hours
and hard work it is to launch your own business

00:05:37.949 --> 00:05:43.460
then…independent filmmakers understand it
in context of the film.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:49.320
They are fine spending 18 hours a day on a
film set for 12 straight days, that’s fine

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:54.870
because they think that’s within the context
of what they do but now it’s broadened.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:06:04.120
But now you have to learn how to broaden the
scope of that to have more skill sets at your

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:07.360
disposal in order to compete in this crazy
market.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:12.730
The pizza shop owner can’t say “Well I
love to make pizza.

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:13.730
That’s my thing.

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:17.980
I’m not going to worry about the bookkeeping
because who cares about that.

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:22.790
I’m not going to worry about the front of
the house or all of that.

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:24.930
All I’m going to do is make my pizza.”

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:29.780
The business owner (the chef may) but the
business owner has to concern themselves with

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:33.320
everything, they have to know the full scope
of everything.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:39.250
They have to be able to put in the hours and
the work to be able to figure that stuff out

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:41.300
if they want a successful business.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:46.290
And even when they do that, most small businesses
fail and they fail primarily because of lack

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:51.970
of quality or they don’t figure those other
things out, they can’t figure out a way

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:53.120
to compete.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:59.932
It’s such an audacious thing to say you
want to be a filmmaker for the rest of of

00:06:59.932 --> 00:07:01.300
your life.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:06.400
This is your dream and what you want to do
is you want to live your life professionally

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.330
making movies for the rest of your life.

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:13.150
That is such a first world, crazy dream to
have.

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:14.419
Which is an awesome dream.

00:07:14.419 --> 00:07:18.300
And if it’s in your heart, 100% go for it.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:24.669
But you have to understand how rare that is
and how audacious that is and the big disconnect

00:07:24.669 --> 00:07:30.151
(especially with independent filmmakers) is
they have this big audacious dream where they

00:07:30.151 --> 00:07:35.949
just want to make their own movies and that’s
their job forever.

00:07:35.949 --> 00:07:43.030
They have this audacious dream but at the
same time don’t want to go this audacious

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:46.840
route to be able to put in the work to be
able to learn all about all the stuff to compete

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:49.030
and make that happen, right?

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:53.419
And what they say to me is “Well, Tarantino
didn’t do that.

00:07:53.419 --> 00:07:54.840
Tarantino broke in.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:58.670
Beyonce doesn’t do this.”

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:04.630
They point to these different people who 1)
broke in in a different era first of all.

00:08:04.630 --> 00:08:07.900
Like we talked about how fast things change
now.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:13.300
Like things have changed from 2019, 2019 to
2015 - completely different.

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:16.910
Let alone when Tarantino broke in in the mid-nineties,
right?

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:20.650
It’s not the same world, it’s not the
same thing.

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:26.820
And the problem I think people have is they
look at Tarantino or they look at Scorsese,

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:28.870
they look at these people “Scorsese doesn’t
do transmedia.

00:08:28.870 --> 00:08:31.830
I don’t want to do transmedia.”

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:38.189
The problem with that is you’re not Scorsese
and it’s kind of a tough yet.

00:08:38.189 --> 00:08:39.630
“You’re not Scorsese…yet.

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:42.009
You’re not Tarantino…yet.”

00:08:42.009 --> 00:08:49.970
Like when I was a kid playing baseball my
dad was my baseball coach and when you teach

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:52.529
kids to play baseball, you teach them to catch
a ball with two hands.

00:08:52.529 --> 00:08:55.910
You teach them to catch with two hands so
the ball won’t pop out of the glove, right?

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:57.249
Real simple thing.

00:08:57.249 --> 00:09:01.839
My dad takes me to a professional baseball
game and I’m watching Ken Griffey, Jr. in

00:09:01.839 --> 00:09:04.069
the outfield catching flyballs.

00:09:04.069 --> 00:09:05.749
He’s catching it with one hand.

00:09:05.749 --> 00:09:07.190
He’s catching it behind his back.

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:10.110
He’s catching it sort of down by the ground.

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:15.319
And I say “Dad, you’re telling me I have
to catch with two hands but Ken Griffey, Jr.

00:09:15.319 --> 00:09:17.129
is not catching with two hands?

00:09:17.129 --> 00:09:19.230
Why can’t I just catch with one hand like
him?”

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:25.369
And he looked at me and said “Son, if once
you get to be as good as Ken Griffey, Jr.

00:09:25.369 --> 00:09:29.649
And once you make the major leagues, you can
do anything you want.

00:09:29.649 --> 00:09:32.730
But until then, you catch it with two hands.”

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:40.670
Right now I think for independent filmmakers
should not map their actions based on the

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:46.319
super successful cream of the crop directors
and producers and writers.

00:09:46.319 --> 00:09:48.360
I think that’s a mistake.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:54.809
As far as craft goes, 100% map your actions
after those people.

00:09:54.809 --> 00:09:58.769
You can learn so much about the craft of filmmaking
from Tarantino obviously.

00:09:58.769 --> 00:10:01.209
But I’m not talking about that.

00:10:01.209 --> 00:10:06.990
Become the creation of the product is just
one component of what it takes to be successful

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:08.189
in the industry.

00:10:08.189 --> 00:10:15.239
So you have to have sort of a collection of
things align and put together to be able to

00:10:15.239 --> 00:10:20.430
have a successful IP [intellectual property],
to be able to have a successful business.

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:23.170
Filmmakers know this, you can make the greatest
movie in the world, if you don’t know how

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:26.350
to distribute it well, no one will see it.

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:30.850
You can be the greatest writer in the world
and write the greatest script but if your

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:36.800
DP doesn’t know what he’s doing or she’s
doing then the film comes out poorly.

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:41.629
It takes more than just your individual talent
to be able to pull this thing off.

00:10:41.629 --> 00:10:46.670
When it comes to craft, map your actions with
the big folks.

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:53.869
But at the same time understand and be self-aware
of where you are in your career and you need

00:10:53.869 --> 00:10:55.399
to say and you can say “You know what?

00:10:55.399 --> 00:11:01.179
One day I can be Tarantino, one day I can
be Scorsese, but I’m not now.”

00:11:01.179 --> 00:11:02.779
What do I use?

00:11:02.779 --> 00:11:03.860
What can I use?

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:10.029
How do I use everything possible in order
to position myself in a way that I can get

00:11:10.029 --> 00:11:11.029
there.

00:11:11.029 --> 00:11:16.920
The path is there but the world is different
than when Steven Spielberg starting shooting

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:18.470
movies, it’s just a different world.

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:23.980
And it’s good to read about Steven Spielberg
and the whole thing but you can’t say “Well

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:26.019
Steven Spielberg didn’t do this, so I don’t
have to do it.”

00:11:26.019 --> 00:11:28.470
Or “I can do it because he did it.”

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:30.569
It’s just a whole different thing.

00:11:30.569 --> 00:11:38.110
I’m very bullish that independent creators
need a multi-platform transmedia super story

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:44.879
more than anybody in order to compete and
in order to make a splash in this oversaturated

00:11:44.879 --> 00:11:45.879
world.

00:11:45.879 --> 00:11:46.679
I don’t think it’s just for the big guys.

